{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2406.14243, what are the four main requirements (R1-R4) for continuous AI auditing, and which architectural services in AuditMAI address each?", "answer": "The four requirements are: R1 identification of relevant artefacts, R2 flexibility of collection, R3 automation, and R4 ease of analysis. Each is addressed by a corresponding AuditMAI architectural service.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "Requirements        | R1 (focus on exploitation phase of ML systems), R2 (log structure was partly pre-defined), R3 (full automation), R4 (query interface with pre-defined queries, detailed custom queries)"}, {"page": 8, "quote": "The four requirements (cf. Section 3.2) are targeted by the following four services: Audit Scoping Service. ... (R1 Identification of relevant auditable artefacts) ... Audit Setup Service. ... (R2 Flexibility of auditable artefact collection mechanisms). ... Artefact Management Service. ... (R3 Automation of auditable artefact management) ... Audit Artefact Analytics and Reporting Service. ... (R4 Ease of analysing auditable artefacts)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2406.14243, how do the two industrial use cases differ in their focus for auditable artefact collection?", "answer": "UC1 focuses on the exploitation phase and runtime traces, while UC2 tracks data flows across the entire system, including consent management and analysis modules.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "In UC 1, the audit focus was put on the exploitation phase capturing runtime phrases of the system, with just a limited number of training parameters being captured, while in UC 2, the focus was on the overall system, also tracking main data flows during execution."}]},
  {"question": "What is the definition of 'AI auditability' as proposed in arXiv 2406.14243?", "answer": "AI auditability is defined as the ability of an auditor to obtain accurate and relevant auditable artefacts for answering concrete audit questions when examining an AI system.", "evidence": [{"page": 3, "quote": "Definition 1 AI auditability is the ability of an auditor to obtain accurate and relevant auditable artefacts for answering concrete audit questions, when examining an AI system."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2406.14243, what are 'dynamic auditable artefacts' and how do they differ from static ones?", "answer": "Dynamic artefacts are traces collected from live systems during training or execution, such as logs or status outputs, while static artefacts are less frequently updated documentation like design decisions.", "evidence": [{"page": 4, "quote": "Dynamic artefacts, including audit traces collected from the live systems, from the training or execution phase of a system (e.g. status or output logs). Furthermore, to avoid opacity and other issues as mentioned in Section 2.1, not all information that can be collected should be collected. Thus, it is essential to consider relevancy and accuracy of auditable artefacts. Both factors are closely linked to the overall audit goal and thus, related audit questions."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2406.14243, which knowledge bases are included in the AuditMAI Knowledge View?", "answer": "The AuditMAI Knowledge View includes knowledge bases for audit questions, documentation standards, and tools and metrics.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "It consists of three knowledge bases: Audit Questions, (cf. Section 2) suggest which information needs to be collected during the audit process. ... Documentation Standards, collect relevant metadata standards to describe and document relevant information of auditable artefacts. ... Tool and Metrics differ depending on task and domain."}]},
  {"question": "Which toolkit is mentioned in arXiv 2406.14243 as providing bias and fairness audit capabilities?", "answer": "Aequitas is mentioned as a toolkit for bias and fairness audit.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Example data sources can be textual descriptions (e.g., Model Cards Mitchell et al. [2019]), automatic MLOps tools (e.g., MLflow 4 ), or specific automatic ones (e.g., Aequitas for fairness Saleiro et al. [2018])."}]}
]}
